6a. A control statement in Java is a statement that controls the flow of a program. It consists of a set of keywords and symbols that define how the program should be executed. 6b. An if statement and an if-else statement in Java are different in that an if statement executes a block of code if a condition is true, whereas an if-else statement executes a block of code if a condition is true and another block of code if the condition is false. 6c. The flow of control in a Java program can be illustrated as follows: 

 if (condition) { 

 // code to be executed if condition is true 

 } else { 

 // code to be executed if condition is false 

 }
